      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm looking at this nda from a fintech client and i'm stuck on the residuals clause. can u explain what this part means in plain english? \n\n\"Notwithstanding the foregoing, the Receiving Party shall be free to use for any purpose the Residuals resulting from access to or use of the Confidential Information, provided that the Receiving Party shall not disclose the Confidential Information. The term 'Residuals' means information in non-tangible form, which may be retained in the unaided memory of persons who have had access to the Confidential Information.\"\n\nbasically, if i learn some specific ways to optimize their sql queries, can i use those same patterns for my other clients later? or does this clause restrict me? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The residuals clause, you're referring to the \"residuals\" in question. It's a bit like a treasure trove of knowledge, but with a catch - you can't just take it and run with it. Think of it like a group of skilled craftsmen, each with their own unique techniques for shaping metal. If you learn one craftsman's methods, you can't just use them to create your own masterpieces without permission. The clause is essentially saying that while you can use the optimized SQL patterns, you can't claim them as your own or use them to compete with the original client.\n\nIn plain English, the clause means that you can use the optimized SQL patterns for your other clients, but you can't disclose the original client's confidential information. It's like having a secret recipe for a delicious dish, but you can't share it with anyone else or use it to open up your own restaurant. You can use the recipe to cook for your other clients, but you have to keep the original recipe a secret.\n\nThe term \"residuals\" is a bit misleading, as it implies that you're retaining some kind of residual value from the original client's confidential information. In reality, it's more like a \"",
